247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (The Arduino Mega is based on the ATmega2560 microcontroller, which is a digital integrated circuit designed for embedded systems applications. This microcontroller integrates a large number of CMOS transistors on a single silicon chip, enabling digital processing, control, and communication. At the hardware level, the ATmega2560 is built using NMOS and PMOS transistors that operate as electronic switches. These transistors are combined to form logic gates, such as AND, OR, NOT, and XOR, which constitute the fundamental building blocks of digital systems. The digital logic implemented inside the microcontroller allows it to process binary signals represented by two logic levels: 0 \(LOW\) and 1 \(HIGH\). Through this logic, the Arduino Mega is able to perform decision-making operations, control digital input/output pins, and manage internal processes such as timing and communication. The Arithmetic Logic Unit \(ALU\) is a core component of the ATmega2560. It executes arithmetic operations including addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, as well as logical and bitwise operations. These functions are implemented using binary arithmetic circuits derived from transistor-based logic gates. The Arduino Mega also includes digital input/output ports controlled by internal registers and transistor drivers. These ports allow the microcontroller to read digital signals from sensors and generate control signals to external devices such as motor drivers and actuators. In embedded and robotic applications, the Arduino Mega processes sensor data using digital logic and arithmetic operations to make real-time decisions. The resulting control signals are then transmitted through digital outputs to drive external hardware. This architecture makes the Arduino Mega a reliable and efficient platform for complex control systems)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
