/P 136 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function configures the direction of all pins using the pinMode\(\) function. Motor control pins are set as outputs so the Arduino can send control signals to the motor driver. Ultrasonic trigger pins are also configured as outputs, while echo pins are set as inputs to receive distance information. Line sensor pins are set as inputs so the Arduino can continuously read their values during operation. Serial communication is initialized to allow observation of sensor values and system behavior during testing and debugging. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (The code includes a dedicated function, readUltrasonicDistance\(\), to handle distance measurement. This function generates a short pulse on the trigger pin, waits for the echo signal on the echo pin, and calculates the distance using the speed of sound. Encapsulating this process in a function makes the code cleaner and avoids repetition. The returned distance value is used later in the decision-making logic of the robot. Motor behavior is controlled through separate movement functions, which improves modularity and clarity. The moveForward\(\) function sets both motors to rotate in the forward direction and applies full PWM speed. The reverse\(\) function changes the motor direction so both motors rotate backward, allowing the robot to move away from the arena boundary. The turnRight\(\) function sets the motors to rotate in opposite directions, causing the robot to rotate around its own axis. These functions translate high-level decisions into low-level motor commands. Inside the main loop\(\) function, the Arduino continuously reads sensor values and evaluates conditions in a specific priority order. Boundary detection has the highest priority to prevent the robot from leaving the arena. If either the left or right line sensor detects the white line, the robot immediately reverses and continues moving backward until the rear sensor confirms it is safely inside the arena. It then turns right to reorient itself. If the rear sensor alone detects the boundary, the robot moves forward slightly and then turns right. When no boundary is detected, the robot evaluates the ultrasonic sensor data. If an object is detected within 30 centimeters, the robot moves forward aggressively. If no object is detected, the robot enters a search mode by continuously turning right. Overall, this code demonstrates structured use of Arduino digital and analog pins, effective sensor integration, and clear decision-making logic. By clearly defining pin assignments, separating functionality into dedicated functions, and prioritizing sensor-based safety behaviors, the program enables the robot to operate autonomously and reliably in a competitive environment.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
