/Title (In an autonomous robotic system, utilizing the FC-51 enables rapid detection of an obstacle or an opponent, facilitating real-time decision making for avoidance or execution of another programmed strategy. The combination of the IR LED, phototransistor, and comparator provides reliable and fast short-range detection, making it a very suitable choice for robotic applications.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 39 0 R
/Prev 785 0 R
/First 1084 0 R
/Last 1084 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1085 0 R
>>
endobj
935 0 obj
<<
/Title (For the maximum distance of 4 m, the time-of-flight is about 23.3 ms, requiring a minimum interval of about 25 ms between pulses. Although for short distances \(40 cm\), the theoretical maximum frequency is high, it is generally recommended to limit the frequency of TRIG pulses to 20 to 50 Hz to ensure measurement stability and prevent interference. Interfacing with the Arduino Mega 2560 is done via four pins: VCC to 5 V, GND to GND, TRIG to a digital pin \(e.g., D9\), and ECHO to another digital pin \(e.g., D10\). Additional details include that precision depends on the angle, color, and texture of the obstacle, that the minimum distance is about 2 cm and the maximum about 4 m, and that the speed of sound is sensitive to temperature and humidity variations. In an autonomous robot like a Sumo Robot, the HC-SR04 is crucial for the rapid detection of an opponent's presence and the accurate calculation of distance to make real-time decisions for attack or avoidance. It is often combined with other sensors \(like the IR FC-51 or TCRT5000\) to improve reliability and precision.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 39 0 R
/Prev 1085 0 R
/First 1086 0 R
/Last 1086 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 786 0 R
>>
endobj
